import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>7.2b — Traversal with Removal: Don't Skip Elements</h1>

<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Basic Application</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h3>Build: Cleanup Tool</h3>
        <p>Removing elements while traversing an ArrayList is tricky — indexes shift! You'll learn the right pattern for filtering out unwanted items.</p>
        <p>Create a class called <code>InventoryCleanup</code> with an <code>ArrayList&lt;String&gt; items</code>:</p>
        <ol>
            <li>Fill with items: "Rusty Sword", "Health Potion", "Broken Shield", "Mana Potion", "Torn Cloak", "Elixir".</li>
            <li>Remove all items containing "Broken" or "Torn" or "Rusty".</li>
            <li><strong>First try:</strong> use a standard for loop with <code>i++</code>. What goes wrong?</li>
            <li><strong>Fix it:</strong> traverse backwards (from size()-1 down to 0). Why does backward traversal work?</li>
            <li><strong>Alternative:</strong> use a while loop with an Iterator (or just check the index carefully).</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h3>The Trap</h3>
        <p>This is one of the most common ArrayList bugs. If you remove index 2, what was at index 3 is now at index 2. If your loop increments i, you skip it. Draw the indexes on paper to see why.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-07/arraylist-basics.html">ArrayList</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-06/array-traversal.html">Array Traversal</a></li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
